        <vevent>
            <method>PUBLISH</method>
            <uid>CFFQDM@@pretalx.com</uid>
            <pentabarf:event-id></pentabarf:event-id>
            <pentabarf:event-slug>-CFFQDM</pentabarf:event-slug>
            <pentabarf:title>OnDemand Andragogy</pentabarf:title>
            <pentabarf:subtitle></pentabarf:subtitle>
            <pentabarf:language>en</pentabarf:language>
            <pentabarf:language-code>en</pentabarf:language-code>
            <dtstart>20260310T110000</dtstart>
            <dtend>20260310T112500</dtend>
            <duration>002500</duration>
            <summary>OnDemand Andragogy</summary>
            <description>Open OnDemand is a web-based platform designed for users to access high-performance computing (HPC) resources, such as supercomputers or clusters, remotely and interactively. Research computing includes frameworks, such as Open OnDemand, that lower the barrier of entry to high-performance computing for users. The abstraction and automation of research workflows enable flexible ways of conducting computational research via solutions that can cater to diverse computing backgrounds and needs, while hiding many of the complexities of resource and data management. However, as new users think of more complex research workflows, the lack of computational knowledge may become a barrier again. Today, generative AI (GenAI) technology offers sophisticated tutoring capabilities and real-time guidance. By combining the capabilities of Open OnDemand with GenAI-powered tools and microservices tailored to institutional computing clusters, information important for decisions, and self-learning experiences can be made more accessible just-in-time when they are needed to enable more research activities. Passenger apps in the Open OnDemand framework may transform the way researchers without strong computational backgrounds approach self-paced learning and enable andragogy for more efficient use of computational and storage resources.


- Examples of helpful utilities and microservices in Open OnDemand

Submit jobs via a user-friendly interface.
Review available resources for advanced job placement and resource requests.
Visualize job efficiency reports.
Submit a request that will be processed automatically.
Encrypt/Decrypt files and folders

- Self-Paced Learning
The GenAI tutor paradigm not only provides information that can debug problems, suggest alternative approaches, and point out relevant online documentation, it also engages users in learning-by-doing through maintaining their self-motivation to problem solve. Besides &quot;chatting&quot; with documentation, GenAI can help generate scenarios, relevant questions, and explanations of correct answers to enable user-defined and self-paced learning. 

- Generative AI and the GenAI Tutor Paradigm
A service with 24/7 availability and accessibility with the goal of helping when users are actively seeking to learn how to solve problems they face. The use of passenger applications could offer a multi-layered approach to HPC andragogy by providing guidance with potential contextual awareness of the specific systems they are deployed in.</description>
            <class>PUBLIC</class>
            <status>CONFIRMED</status>
            <category>Talk</category>
            <url>https://pretalx.com/good-2026/talk/CFFQDM/</url>
            <location>Main Hall</location>
            
            <attendee>Kyriakos Tsoukalas [Colgate University]</attendee>
            
        </vevent>
        
        <vevent>
            <method>PUBLISH</method>
            <uid>AWC8QJ@@pretalx.com</uid>
            <pentabarf:event-id></pentabarf:event-id>
            <pentabarf:event-slug>-AWC8QJ</pentabarf:event-slug>
            <pentabarf:title>Building LLM Inference Services for Researchers on HPC with Open OnDemand</pentabarf:title>
            <pentabarf:subtitle></pentabarf:subtitle>
            <pentabarf:language>en</pentabarf:language>
            <pentabarf:language-code>en</pentabarf:language-code>
            <dtstart>20260310T113000</dtstart>
            <dtend>20260310T115500</dtend>
            <duration>002500</duration>
            <summary>Building LLM Inference Services for Researchers on HPC with Open OnDemand</summary>
            <description>In this talk, we describe our efforts to provide LLM inference services for researchers at Ohio Supercomputer Center (OSC). Many of our users want to run LLMs, but they have a wide variety of workflows and tool preferences. Our goal has been to support these needs in a simple and reliable way, using Open OnDemand as the main entry point. 

We started by installing an Ollama module on our HPC cluster that includes OpenWebUI and a few wrappers for easy use. Users can start an OnDemand virtual desktop session, download models, start an Ollama server, and run OpenWebUI to access the interface in a browser. At this point, it should support all the workflows users need, but it is still not accessible enough for most users. 

To make this more accessible, we built a dedicated OnDemand app for Ollama. When launched, it starts an Ollama server for the user and opens a browser with the OpenWebUI interface. This made the system much more accessible, although it does not scale well because each user session runs on its own compute node. 

We also created a Jupyter + Ollama app. This app does not use OpenWebUI. Instead, it launches a Jupyter session with an Ollama server running in the background. Through a Jupyter kernel, users can access the OpenAI API interface. This works well for users who want to test the code and run inference batch jobs. 

To reduce friction further, we updated our module so that several popular models are stored in a central location. Users can start quickly without downloading anything, but they can still download additional models into their home or project directories. 

We have also deployed generative AI services on our Kubernetes cluster. This includes both LLM inference and a stable diffusion service. These run well for small numbers of users, but the cost of running many user sessions is high, and we do not yet have a scalable deployment model. A major part of our future work is building a more scalable LLM service on Kubernetes. To date, we have deployed a core set of tools (Ollama, OpenWebUI, and OpenAI API) and plan to expand these options. Initial tests have shown very promising results.</description>
            <class>PUBLIC</class>
            <status>CONFIRMED</status>
            <category>Talk</category>
            <url>https://pretalx.com/good-2026/talk/AWC8QJ/</url>
            <location>Main Hall</location>
            
            <attendee>Heechang Na [Ohio Supercomputer Center]</attendee>
            
        </vevent>
        
